      "writtenOn": "2017-01-20T01:49:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, good points.\n\nEchoing what you said, I think there are a couple things to consider:\ni) if we don\u0027t clear the buffer, does the old data interfere with the new data? (premise: the newest connection attempt is the most important)\nii) if we _do_ clear the buffer, are there cases where we\u0027d miss important data?\n\nOn i), it\u0027s important to note that, when the trace buffer fills up, the kernel drops the oldest events. So we don\u0027t need to worry that skipping the clear will cause the new connection to go untraced.\n\nOn ii), I wouldn\u0027t be too surprised if there are cases where we spuriously believe that a new connection has started. Of course, that\u0027s not a strong argument for retaining the buffer, since I don\u0027t have a concrete example.\n\nOn balance, I think it makes sense to keep the buffer. (Keeping the buffer helps with the possible case ii), and doesn\u0027t make case i) bad.)",
      "parentUuid": "082efe90_fe45a006",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 68,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 69,
        "endChar": 32
      },
